\title{Tie Ceilings of the Trailing-Zero Statistic:\\
the $6/7$ Law, a Catalan Defect, and a Half-Weight Phase Boundary}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the maximal Spearman rank correlation attainable by a coarse integer statistic, as a
function of the law generating its inputs. The statistic of interest is the trailing-zero count
$T(x)=\nu_2(x)$ of a $b$-bit word --- the $2$-adic valuation --- used as a cheap predictive
``dial''. Because a rank statistic can never resolve within a tie class, the tie profile of $T$
imposes an exact arithmetic ceiling
$\rmax(m)=1-\frac{\sum_j (m_j^3-m_j)}{n^3-n}$
on any Spearman correlation the dial can achieve. We compute this ceiling exactly under two
families of draw laws and determine the complete comparison between them. Under a \emph{uniform}
draw on $b$ bits the tie profile of $T$ is dyadic and the ceiling equals
$\frac67\bigl(1+\frac{1}{2^b(2^b+1)}\bigr)$, strictly above $6/7$ and converging to it at rate
$4^{-b}$. Under a \emph{fixed-weight} draw the tie profile is a hockey stick of binomial
coefficients $\binom{b-1-k}{w-1}$. For the balanced law $b=2v+2$, $w=v+1$ the leading blocks are
$m_0=(2v+1)\Cat_v$ and $m_1=(v+1)\Cat_v$, so the shortfall of the first step from exact halving is
precisely the $v$-th Catalan number, $2m_1-m_0=\Cat_v$; we prove the unconditional bracket
$\frac67-\frac{1}{15(v+1)}<\rmax<\frac67$ for $v\ge2$, with equality at $v=1$, so that $6/7$ is a
two-sided attractor separating the two draw laws at every bit length. Across the fixed-weight
family we establish a sharp dichotomy: the ceiling is at most $6/7$ if and only if the weight is
at least half the bit length, with excess at least $\frac{1}{7(2v+3)}$ on the sparse side. The
order principle behind every comparison is isolated: at fixed sample size the ceiling is strictly
antitone in the cube sum, so a single transfer between tie blocks strictly lowers it. Finally,
over a $q$-letter alphabet the universal constant is $\frac{3q}{q^2+q+1}$, strictly decreasing in
$q$, of which $6/7$ is the binary instance; consequently an acceptance band with top end $0.85$ is
binary-specific. We apply the results to a recorded $60$-bit measurement
($\rho=0.669$, interval $[0.634,0.705]$, advantage $+0.151$ over a popcount baseline): the entire
acceptance band lies strictly below both ceilings, the dial is not saturated, and the advantage
runs against the tie-headroom ordering on uniform draws while being structurally forced on
fixed-weight draws.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}

A \emph{dial} is a cheap statistic used to predict an expensive outcome. The dial studied here is
the trailing-zero count of a machine word,
\[
T(x)=\nu_2(x)=\max\{k:2^k\mid x\},
\]
the position of the lowest set bit. A controlled study on uniformly drawn $60$-bit words recorded a
Spearman rank correlation $\rho(T,\text{rate})=0.669$ with interval $[0.634,0.705]$, inside a
pre-registered acceptance band $[0.55,0.85]$, together with an advantage of $+0.151$
(interval $[0.107,0.193]$) over a popcount baseline.

The methodological question is: \emph{compared to what?} A rank correlation is interpretable only
against the maximum the statistic could attain, and for a coarse statistic that maximum is strictly
below $1$ and depends on the input law. This paper computes it.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Exact ceilings for uniform (dyadic) and fixed-weight (hockey-stick) draw laws.
\item The Catalan spine: the deviation of the balanced law from the dyadic law is one step of the
tie profile, of size exactly a Catalan number.
\item A two-sided attractor: $6/7$ separates the two laws at every bit length.
\item A half-weight phase boundary across the whole weight axis, with a quantitative gap.
\item A transfer principle: the ceiling is strictly antitone in the cube sum at fixed total.
\item The radix law: the universal constant is $3q/(q^2+q+1)$, strictly decreasing in $q$.
\item Deployment consequences for the recorded $60$-bit measurement.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Tie profiles and the ceiling functional}

\begin{definition}[Tie profile]
Let $S$ be a statistic on a finite population of size $n$. Its \emph{tie profile} is the list
$m=(m_0,\dots,m_k)$ of cardinalities of the level sets of $S$, with $n=|m|=\sum_j m_j$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Tie correction and ceiling functional]
For a profile $m$ put $\mathcal T(m)=\frac1{12}\sum_j(m_j^3-m_j)$ and, for $n\ge2$,
\[
\rmax(m)=1-\frac{12\,\mathcal T(m)}{n^3-n}=1-\frac{\sum_j m_j^3-n}{n^3-n},
\qquad C(m):=\sum_j m_j^3 .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Interpretation]
$\rmax(m)$ is the supremum of the squared tie-corrected Spearman correlation between a statistic
with tie profile $m$ and any response. It lies in $[0,1]$, equals $1$ iff all $m_j=1$, and equals
$0$ iff $m$ is the single block $(n)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With mid-ranks inside blocks, the rank vector of $S$ has variance
$\frac{n^3-n}{12}-\mathcal T(m)$ rather than $\frac{n^3-n}{12}$. Cauchy--Schwarz against an untied
response gives the stated ratio of variances as the squared correlation bound, attained by any
response strictly increasing across blocks.
\end{proof}

Elementary evaluations used below:
$\rmax(3,2,1)=\frac67$, $\rmax(4,3,2,1)=\frac{10}{11}$, $\rmax(2,1)=\frac34$, $\rmax(n)=0$.

\begin{theorem}[Antitonicity in the cube sum]\label{thm:anti}
If $|m|=|m'|=n\ge2$ and $C(m)\le C(m')$ then $\rmax(m')\le\rmax(m)$, strictly if $C(m)<C(m')$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$C\mapsto 1-\frac{C-n}{n^3-n}$ is strictly decreasing.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Transfer principle]\label{thm:transfer}
Let $a+1\le b$ and let $L$ be any profile. Then
$C\bigl((a+1),b,L\bigr)<C\bigl(a,(b+1),L\bigr)$ and hence
$\rmax\bigl(a,(b+1),L\bigr)<\rmax\bigl((a+1),b,L\bigr)$: moving one observation from a smaller tie
block into a larger one strictly lowers the ceiling.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The cube sum changes by $(3b^2+3b+1)-(3a^2+3a+1)>0$ since $x\mapsto 3x^2+3x+1$ is strictly
increasing on $x\ge0$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:anti}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Two blocks]
$C(a,b)=(a+b)^3-3ab(a+b)$, so at fixed total the cube sum strictly decreases in the product $ab$.
Hence for $a'\le a\le b\le b'$ with $a+b=a'+b'$ we get $\rmax(a',b')\le\rmax(a,b)$, and the flat
split maximises the ceiling.
\end{corollary}

At $n=12$: $\rmax(6,6)=0.755245$, $(5,7)=0.734266$, $(4,8)=0.671329$, $(3,9)=0.566434$,
$(2,10)=0.419580$, $(1,11)=0.230769$. Concentration of ties destroys rank resolution.

\section{Uniform draws: the dyadic profile and $6/7$}

\begin{definition}
For $b\ge1$ let $D_b=(2^{b-1},2^{b-2},\dots,2,1,1)$, of total $2^b$: the tie profile of $\nu_2$ on
uniform $b$-bit words (the last block is the singleton class of the zero word).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact uniform ceiling]\label{thm:dyadic}
For $b\ge1$, with $N=2^b$,
\[
\rmax(D_b)=\frac{6}{7}\left(1+\frac{1}{N(N+1)}\right)>\frac67,
\qquad \rmax(D_b)-\frac67<4^{-b}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$C(D_b)=1+\sum_{k=0}^{b-1}8^{\,b-1-k}=\frac{N^3-1}{7}+1$, so
\[
\rmax(D_b)=1-\frac{\frac{N^3-1}{7}+1-N}{N^3-N}
=\frac{6(N^3-1)}{7N(N^2-1)}=\frac{6(N^2+N+1)}{7N(N+1)},
\]
and $\frac{N^2+N+1}{N(N+1)}=1+\frac{1}{N(N+1)}$.
\end{proof}

The constant is the geometric limit: a profile of ratio $1/2$ has cube-sum ratio $1/8$ and burns
$\frac{1}{1+2+4}=\frac17$ of the rank budget. Equivalently $7=3c^2+3c+1$ at $c=1$. At $b=60$ the
correction is $\approx7\times10^{-37}$.

\section{Fixed-weight draws: the hockey-stick profile}

\begin{definition}
For $0\le w\le b$ let $W(b,w)$ be the set of $b$-bit words of Hamming weight $w$, identified with
the $w$-subsets of $\{0,\dots,b-1\}$; the trailing-zero statistic is $T(S)=\min S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Block sizes]\label{thm:blocks}
For $k<b$ the number of $S\in W(b,w)$ with $\min S=k$ is exactly $\binom{b-1-k}{w-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix the element $k$, exclude everything below it, and choose the remaining $w-1$ elements freely
among the $b-1-k$ positions above $k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Hockey-stick profile]
Parametrise $w=v+1$, $b=v+1+r$. Put
$H(v,r)=\bigl(\binom{v+r}{v},\binom{v+r-1}{v},\dots,\binom{v}{v}\bigr)$, i.e.
$H(v,r)_k=\binom{b-1-k}{w-1}$ for $0\le k\le r$. The \emph{weight fraction} is
$\theta=\frac{w}{b}=\frac{v+1}{v+1+r}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Hockey-stick identity]
$|H(v,r)|=\sum_{k=0}^{r}\binom{v+r-k}{v}=\binom{v+1+r}{v+1}=\binom{b}{w}$, so $H(v,r)$ is indeed the
tie profile of $T$ on $W(b,w)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{definition}
$B_v:=H(v,v+1)$ is the \emph{balanced} profile at $b=2v+2$, $w=v+1$; e.g. $B_1=(3,2,1)$,
$B_2=(10,6,3,1)$, $B_3=(35,20,10,4,1)$, $B_4=(126,70,35,15,5,1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Exact step ratio]\label{lem:ratio}
Consecutive blocks satisfy $\binom{v+j-1}{v}\big/\binom{v+j}{v}=\frac{j}{v+j}$; the ratio increases
with the block index $j$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Halving below the top]\label{lem:halve}
If $j\le v$ then $\frac{j}{v+j}\le\frac12$. Consequently, for $r\le v$,
$C\bigl(H(v,r)\bigr)\le\frac87 m_0^3$ with $m_0=\binom{v+r}{v}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Geometric envelope]\label{lem:geom}
For $v\ge1$ and all $r\ge0$, with $m_0=\binom{v+r}{v}$ and $q=\frac{r}{v+r}$,
\[
C\bigl(H(v,r)\bigr)\bigl((v+r)^3-r^3\bigr)\;\le\; m_0^3(v+r)^3,
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad C\le\frac{m_0^3}{1-q^3}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Head-to-total ratio]\label{lem:head}
$|H(v,r)|\,(v+1)=m_0\,(v+r+1)$; the head occupies the fraction $\frac{v+1}{v+r+1}$ of the sample.
\end{lemma}

\section{The Catalan spine and the balanced ceiling}

Write $\Cat_v=\frac{1}{v+1}\binom{2v}{v}$.

\begin{theorem}[Catalan spine]\label{thm:catalan}
The two leading blocks of $B_v$ are $m_0=\binom{2v+1}{v}=(2v+1)\Cat_v$ and
$m_1=\binom{2v}{v}=(v+1)\Cat_v$; hence
\[
2m_1-m_0=\Cat_v .
\]
Moreover $m_1=2m_2$ exactly and $|B_v|=2m_0=2(2v+1)\Cat_v$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\binom{2v}{v}=(v+1)\Cat_v$ is the definition; $(v+1)\binom{2v+1}{v}=(2v+1)\binom{2v}{v}$ gives
$m_0=(2v+1)\Cat_v$, and $2m_1-m_0=(2v+2-2v-1)\Cat_v$. The identity
$\binom{2u+2}{u+1}=2\binom{2u+1}{u+1}$ gives $m_1=2m_2$ and $|B_v|=2m_0$.
\end{proof}

For $v=1,2,3,4$ the defect $2m_1-m_0$ is $1,2,5,14$: the Catalan numbers. The balanced law differs
from the dyadic law only in its first step, and the difference has relative size
$\Cat_v/m_0=\frac{1}{2v+1}$.

\begin{theorem}[Lower bound]\label{thm:lower}
For all $v\ge1$, $\;\rmax(B_v)>\frac67-\frac{1}{15(v+1)}$; the cruder bound
$\rmax(B_v)>\frac67-\frac1{v+1}$ holds for all $v\ge0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Split $B_v=(m_0,H(v,v))$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:halve}, $C(B_v)\le m_0^3+\frac87m_1^3$, while the total
is $n=2m_0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:catalan}. Substituting into the definition of $\rmax$ and
eliminating $m_1=\frac{v+1}{2v+1}m_0$ reduces the claim to a polynomial positivity statement in $v$
and $m_0\ge1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound, unconditional]\label{thm:upper}
For every $v\ge2$, $\;\rmax(B_v)<\frac67$. At $v=1$ equality holds: $\rmax(3,2,1)=\frac67$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch, and why it is delicate]
The geometric estimate of Lemma~\ref{lem:halve} is exactly tight against $6/7$, so no fixed
truncation succeeds uniformly: expanding the top $K$ blocks exactly and bounding the rest
geometrically gains $O(8^{-K})$, while the whole strict inequality is carried by the Catalan defect
of relative size $\frac{1}{2v+1}$; a $K$-block argument thus reaches only $v\lesssim 8^K$. (With the
three leading blocks $m_0,m_1=2m_2,m_2$ one obtains the bound exactly where
$63(v+1)^3>8(2v+1)^3$, i.e. $v\le94$.)

The uniform argument never truncates: one proves by induction \emph{down the entire profile} an
accumulated-deficit invariant comparing each suffix's cube sum with the geometric ideal
$\frac87m^3$, with coefficient $\frac{24(1+7(v-r))}{49(v+1)}$ linear in the remaining depth. That
coefficient is the fixed point of the recursion $e_{s-1}=(s-1)+e_s/8$, which is why the inductive
step closes with equality; the exponentially small tail is absorbed by an additive constant.
Combining the invariant at full depth with the head ratio and $m_0\ge v(2v+1)$ yields strictness
for all $v\ge2$.
\end{proof}

Exact values: $\rmax(B_1)=\frac67$, $\rmax(B_2)=\frac{563}{665}=0.846616\ldots$,
$\rmax(B_3)=\frac{1386}{1633}=0.848744\ldots$, $\rmax(B_4)=0.850682\ldots$,
$\rmax(B_{29})=0.856239\ldots$ (bit length $60$), $\rmax(B_{94})=0.856864\ldots$

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided attractor]\label{thm:sandwich}
For every $v\ge2$, at $b=2v+2$,
\[
\rmax(B_v)<\frac67<\rmax(D_{2v+2}),
\qquad
0<\rmax(D_{2v+2})-\rmax(B_v)<\frac{1}{15(v+1)}+4^{-(2v+2)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:dyadic}, \ref{thm:lower}, \ref{thm:upper}.
\end{proof}

Thus $6/7$ is not merely the common limit of the two laws: it separates them, exponentially from
above and at rate $\Theta(1/v)$ from below.

\section{The weight axis: a single phase boundary}

\begin{theorem}[Dense side]\label{thm:dense}
For $1\le r\le v$ with $\binom{v+r}{v}\ge3$, $\;\rmax\bigl(H(v,r)\bigr)<\frac67$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Feed the accumulated-deficit invariant and Lemma~\ref{lem:head} into the definition of $\rmax$.
With $P=v+1$ and $D=v-r+1\ge1$, the surplus polynomial is
$N=420DP^2+144P^2-294PD^2+49D^3$, positive because $10P\ge7D$, and it dominates the linear terms
once the head block has size at least $3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Half-weight boundary]\label{thm:boundary}
Let $v\ge1$ and $1\le r\le v+1$, i.e. $2w\ge b$. Then $\rmax\bigl(H(v,r)\bigr)\le\frac67$, with
equality exactly at the balanced law of bit length $4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $r\le v$ this is Theorem~\ref{thm:dense}, except at the corner $v=r=1$ where the profile is
$(2,1)$ with ceiling $\frac34$. For $r=v+1$ it is Theorem~\ref{thm:upper} for $v\ge2$ and the exact
value $\frac67$ at $v=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sparse side, quantitative]\label{thm:sparse}
For $v\ge1$ and $r\ge v+2$, i.e. $2w<b$,
\[
\rmax\bigl(H(v,r)\bigr)\;>\;\frac67+\frac{1}{7(2v+3)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Insert Lemmas~\ref{lem:geom} and~\ref{lem:head} into the definition of $\rmax$. The claim reduces to
\[
7(v+1)^3(v+r)^3<(v+r+1)^3\bigl((v+r)^3-r^3\bigr),
\]
whose difference, after $v=a+1$, $r=a+3+s$ with $a,s\ge0$, has strictly positive coefficients.
\end{proof}

Along $r=v+2$ the exact values satisfy $v(\rmax-\frac67)\to\frac{54}{343}=0.1574\ldots$, while the
proved bound gives $\to\frac1{14}=0.0714\ldots$

\begin{theorem}[Dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
For $v\ge1$, $r\ge1$:
\[
\rmax\bigl(H(v,r)\bigr)>\frac67 \iff r\ge v+2 \iff 2w<b .
\]
The sign of $\rmax-\frac67$ depends on the weight fraction alone, and the unique phase boundary sits
exactly at half weight.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
At $v=1$, $r=3$ (weight $2$, bit length $5$) the profile is $(4,3,2,1)$ and
$\rmax=\frac{10}{11}>\frac67$: the boundary cannot be moved.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[Why the threshold is at half weight]
Put $r=cv$ and let $v\to\infty$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:head} the head fraction tends to
$\frac{1}{1+c}$ and by Lemma~\ref{lem:ratio} the largest step ratio tends to $q=\frac{c}{1+c}$, so
the burnt fraction of the rank budget tends to
$\frac{1}{(1+c)^3}\cdot\frac{1}{1-q^3}=\frac{1}{(1+c)^3-c^3}$. The ceiling therefore exceeds $6/7$
exactly when
\[
(1+c)^3-c^3=3c^2+3c+1>7 \iff c>1,
\]
which is the half-weight line. The $7$ of $6/7$ and the location of the phase boundary are the same
fact about cubes.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Imbalance robustness]\label{thm:imbalance}
If $1\le r\le v$ and $2(v+1)\le3r$ --- equivalently $\frac12\le\theta\le\frac35$ --- then
$\rmax\bigl(H(v,r)\bigr)>\frac{73}{100}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The hypotheses force $5m_0\le3n$ via Lemma~\ref{lem:head}; with $C\le\frac87m_0^3$ from
Lemma~\ref{lem:halve} this gives $C\le\frac{216}{875}n^3$, whence $\rmax>1-\frac{27}{100}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every fixed-weight law with $\theta\in[\frac12,\frac35]$ we have
$\frac{73}{100}<\rmax\le\frac67$, so every $\rho\in[0.55,0.85]$ satisfies
$\rho^2\le0.7225<\rmax$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The alphabet: what $6/7$ is a function of}

\begin{definition}
For $q\ge2$ and $b\ge1$ let $R_{q,b}=\bigl((q-1)q^{b-1},\dots,(q-1),1\bigr)$, the tie profile of the
base-$q$ valuation on uniform length-$b$ strings; total $q^b$. At $q=2$ this is $D_b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Radix ceiling]\label{thm:radix}
For $q\ge2$, $b\ge1$, with $N=q^b$,
\[
\rmax(R_{q,b})=\frac{3q}{q^2+q+1}\left(1+\frac{1}{N(N+1)}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$C(R_{q,b})=1+(q-1)^3\frac{N^3-1}{q^3-1}=1+\frac{(q-1)^2(N^3-1)}{q^2+q+1}$; substituting and
factoring as in Theorem~\ref{thm:dyadic} gives the constant
$1-\frac{(q-1)^2}{q^2+q+1}=\frac{3q}{q^2+q+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict antitonicity in the alphabet size]
$q\mapsto\frac{3q}{q^2+q+1}$ is strictly decreasing for $q\ge2$:
$\frac67\approx0.857$, $\frac9{13}\approx0.692$, $\frac{12}{21}\approx0.571$ for $q=2,3,4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The band is binary-specific]
For $q\ge3$ and $b\ge2$, $\;\rmax(R_{q,b})\le\frac{7}{10}<0.85^2$. An acceptance band whose top end
is $0.85$ is therefore unattainable, hence unfalsifiable at its top end, over any non-binary
alphabet.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\frac{3q}{q^2+q+1}\le\frac9{13}$ for $q\ge3$ and the finite-length correction is at most
$1+\frac1{90}$ for $N\ge9$; $\frac9{13}\cdot\frac{91}{90}=\frac7{10}$.
\end{proof}

The denominator $q^2+q+1=\frac{q^3-1}{q-1}$ is once again $(1+c)^3-c^3$ in disguise: the transfer
gain, the phase boundary and the radix constant are one fact about cubes.

\section{Application: reading a 60-bit deployment measurement}

The recorded study: uniform draws at bit length $60$, $\rho=0.669$ with interval $[0.634,0.705]$,
acceptance band $[0.55,0.85]$, advantage over popcount $+0.151$ with interval $[0.107,0.193]$;
implied popcount reading $0.518$.

\begin{proposition}[Band admissibility]
Every $\rho\in[0.55,0.85]$ satisfies $\rho^2<\rmax(B_{29})$ and $\rho^2<\rmax(D_{60})$, where
$\rmax(B_{29})=0.856239\ldots<\frac67<\rmax(D_{60})=\frac67+7\times10^{-37}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$0.85^2=0.7225<\frac67-\frac1{450}$; apply Theorems~\ref{thm:dyadic}, \ref{thm:lower},
\ref{thm:upper}.
\end{proof}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:imbalance} the same holds for every weight fraction in $[\frac12,\frac35]$
(e.g. weight $33$ on $60$ bits, exact ceiling $0.81611\ldots$), and by Theorem~\ref{thm:sparse} a
fortiori on the entire sparse side.

\begin{proposition}[No saturation]
The observed $\rho^2=0.4476$ uses $52\%$ of the ceiling $\approx0.857$: the reading is not
ceiling-limited, and the residual gap is informational rather than arithmetic.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Popcount collapse]
Under any fixed-weight draw law the popcount is constant, its tie profile is the single block
$\bigl(\binom{b}{w}\bigr)$, and its ceiling is exactly $0$, while the trailing-zero dial retains
$\rmax\ge0.85$ at $b=60$, $w=30$. On the balanced half of the envelope the advantage of $T$ is
structurally forced.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The advantage is not a headroom artefact]
At $b=60$ the popcount tie profile $\bigl(\binom{60}{k}\bigr)_{k=0}^{60}$ has ceiling
$0.99391\ldots>\frac67=\rmax(D_{60})$. The baseline therefore has strictly \emph{more} tie headroom
than $T$ and still loses by $0.151$: the advantage runs against the headroom ordering and cannot be
produced by tie granularity.
\end{theorem}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Exact ceiling of a profile.} Return $1-\frac{\sum m_j^3-n}{n^3-n}$ in exact rational
arithmetic; $O(k)$ big-integer cubes for $k$ blocks.

\paragraph{B. Profile construction.} Dyadic $m_k=2^{b-1-k}$ plus a singleton; hockey stick
$m_k=\binom{b-1-k}{w-1}$ generated by the exact step recursion $m_{j-1}/m_j=j/(v+j)$; radix
$m_k=(q-1)q^{b-1-k}$ plus a singleton.

\paragraph{C. Phase-boundary certification.} For fixed $v$, scan $r$ and record the sign of
$\rmax(H(v,r))-\frac67$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy} it flips exactly between $r=v+1$ and
$r=v+2$.

\paragraph{D. Transfer descent.} Repeatedly move a unit from a smaller block to a larger one; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:transfer} the ceiling strictly decreases, terminating at $0$; the reverse ascent
terminates at the flat profile, which maximises the ceiling.

\section{Discussion}

For a coarse rank predictor, $1$ is the wrong reference point; the right one is $\rmax$, a
computable function of the pair (statistic, draw law). Three practical rules follow: report the
ceiling with the correlation; remember that ceilings move when the generator moves (changing from
uniform to balanced draws moves it by $9\times10^{-4}$ at $60$ bits, but moving below half weight
moves it across $6/7$, and moving to a non-binary alphabet moves it below $0.7$); and compare like
with like, since a baseline may lose because it is less informative or because the draw law has
annihilated it.

Structurally, everything is driven by $(x+1)^3-x^3=3x^2+3x+1$: it makes the ceiling antitone under
transfers, its value at $c=1$ is the $7$ of $6/7$, the inequality $3c^2+3c+1>7$ locates the phase
boundary at half weight, and $q^2+q+1$ is the denominator of the radix constant. The one different
ingredient is the Catalan spine, which explains why the balanced deviation is inward.

\paragraph{Limitations.} The ceiling bounds attainable correlation but does not predict the attained
value; the constant $1/15$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:lower} is not optimal (the true deficit is
$\approx0.0263/v$); the degenerate corners of the fixed-weight family are excluded as trivial.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Determine $\lim_{v\to\infty}v\bigl(\frac67-\rmax(B_v)\bigr)$ in closed form (numerically
$\approx0.0263$) and sharpen Theorem~\ref{thm:lower} accordingly.
\item Prove a general bracketing theorem for profiles with monotone step ratios, subsuming
Lemmas~\ref{lem:halve}, \ref{lem:geom} and Theorems~\ref{thm:dense}, \ref{thm:sparse}.
\item Formulate Theorem~\ref{thm:transfer} as Schur-concavity of $\rmax$ and identify which
comparisons here are majorization comparisons.
\item Establish the fixed-composition analogue of the half-weight boundary over a $q$-letter
alphabet; the natural conjecture is a boundary at composition fraction $1/q$.
\item Study joint ceilings for pairs of coarse statistics via the tie profile of joint level sets.
\item Solve the inverse problem: characterise the set of achievable ceilings over a natural class of
draw laws.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The trailing-zero statistic has tie ceiling $\frac67\bigl(1+\frac1{2^b(2^b+1)}\bigr)$ under uniform
$b$-bit draws and a hockey-stick ceiling under fixed-weight draws, the two being separated by $6/7$
at every bit length, with the separation created by a single Catalan-sized step. Across the weight
axis the ceiling crosses $6/7$ exactly at half weight; across alphabets the constant is
$\frac{3q}{q^2+q+1}$. All of this follows from one order principle --- the ceiling is strictly
antitone in the cube sum at fixed sample size --- and one cubic identity. Applied to the $60$-bit
measurement, the results show that the whole acceptance band is arithmetically admissible, that the
reading is far from saturation, and that the advantage over the popcount baseline is real on
uniform draws and forced on balanced ones.

